---
/**
 * The three linework icons in the "How it works" columns.
 *
 * **They draw themselves in.** Every shape carries `pathLength="1"`, which
 * normalises its geometry to a unit length whatever it actually measures — so
 * one `stroke-dasharray: 1; stroke-dashoffset: 1 → 0` rule animates a rect, a
 * circle and a bezier identically, with no per-path length measured in JS. The
 * transition fires off the `.is-in` class the page's observer already puts on
 * the column, so this adds **zero** script. That observer is two-way, so the
 * drawing unwinds on the way up and redraws on the way back down.
 *
 * The box is `64` units drawn at `80px`, and the stroke is `1.5` so that the
 * rendered weight lands where the old 48-unit/56px box put it. The size is what
 * buys the detail below: three stacks, a six-node pipeline and a seven-node
 * graph are all illegible smudges at 56px and read as diagrams at 80.
 *
 * Three rules the geometry has to obey, each of which an early draft broke:
 *
 *  - **Nothing overlaps.** Two strokes crossing in a small box merge into a
 *    smudge — the first tables icon put one card on top of another and the
 *    shared corner read as a printing error. So the stacks here are dense but
 *    never overlapping: the gap within a stack is half the gap between stacks,
 *    which is what makes three columns of cards read as three *stacks* rather
 *    than as a grid. The one deliberate exception is an arrowhead sitting on
 *    the last unit of its own connector, which is a join and not a collision.
 *  - **Every line ends somewhere.** The graph had a satellite-to-satellite edge
 *    that crossed the whole box and looked like a scratch. Every stroke here
 *    starts on one object's edge and stops on another's — the radials meet the
 *    hub and the satellites exactly on their circumferences, and the three
 *    chords run between adjacent satellites only, so no edge crosses another.
 *  - **Connectors are orthogonal, with one arrowhead each.** The first pipeline
 *    used rounded corner curves and a floating chevron; right angles and a
 *    single head at the point of entry read as a diagram instead of as debris.
 *    Where two connectors enter the same node they enter at different points on
 *    its edge, because two arrowheads landing on one point is the smudge again.
 *
 * Held to the page's ranking rule: one ink colour, one stroke weight, one size,
 * for all three. These sit in columns LANDING.md forbids from ranking, and an
 * icon that ranked would rank them. It is why the three stacks are the same
 * height and hold the same number of cards — stacks of unequal height in a row
 * are a bar chart, and a bar chart is a ranking.
 *
 * The declaration order is the draw order — containers first, then what joins
 * them — because a pipeline whose arrows arrive before its boxes reads as noise.
 */
interface Props {
  /** `icon` from `principles.yaml`. */
  name: "tables" | "pipeline" | "graph";
}
const { name } = Astro.props;
---

<svg
  class="p-icon"
  viewBox="0 0 64 64"
  fill="none"
  stroke="currentColor"
  stroke-width="1.5"
  stroke-linecap="round"
  stroke-linejoin="round"
  aria-hidden="true"
>
  {
    /* 01 — tables. Three stacks of four, drawn stack by stack, because a data
       scientist's problem is never one table and rarely one source. The top
       card of each stack carries a header rule: what gets measured is the
       contents, and the stack behind it is the rest of the same shape. */
    name === "tables" && (
      <>
        <rect x="2" y="8" width="15" height="10" rx="1.5" pathLength="1" />
        <rect x="2" y="20.5" width="15" height="10" rx="1.5" pathLength="1" />
        <rect x="2" y="33" width="15" height="10" rx="1.5" pathLength="1" />
        <rect x="2" y="45.5" width="15" height="10" rx="1.5" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="M2 11.5h15" pathLength="1" />
        <rect x="24.5" y="8" width="15" height="10" rx="1.5" pathLength="1" />
        <rect x="24.5" y="20.5" width="15" height="10" rx="1.5" pathLength="1" />
        <rect x="24.5" y="33" width="15" height="10" rx="1.5" pathLength="1" />
        <rect x="24.5" y="45.5" width="15" height="10" rx="1.5" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="M24.5 11.5h15" pathLength="1" />
        <rect x="47" y="8" width="15" height="10" rx="1.5" pathLength="1" />
        <rect x="47" y="20.5" width="15" height="10" rx="1.5" pathLength="1" />
        <rect x="47" y="33" width="15" height="10" rx="1.5" pathLength="1" />
        <rect x="47" y="45.5" width="15" height="10" rx="1.5" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="M47 11.5h15" pathLength="1" />
      </>
    )
  }

  {
    /* 02 — the pipeline. Three sources, two steps that stand on them, one
       result: depth, because the point is that each stage is built on what the
       stage before it measured. Every connector is orthogonal and arrives at an
       arrowhead on the edge of the node it enters. */
    name === "pipeline" && (
      <>
        <rect x="2" y="4" width="13" height="10" rx="2" pathLength="1" />
        <rect x="2" y="27" width="13" height="10" rx="2" pathLength="1" />
        <rect x="2" y="50" width="13" height="10" rx="2" pathLength="1" />
        <rect x="26" y="12" width="13" height="12" rx="2" pathLength="1" />
        <rect x="26" y="40" width="13" height="12" rx="2" pathLength="1" />
        <rect x="49" y="26" width="13" height="12" rx="2" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="M15 9h5.5V15H26" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="M15 32h5.5V21H26" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="M15 55h5.5V46H26" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="M39 18h5.5V29H49" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="M39 46h5.5V35H49" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="m23.5 12.5 2.5 2.5-2.5 2.5" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="m23.5 18.5 2.5 2.5-2.5 2.5" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="m23.5 43.5 2.5 2.5-2.5 2.5" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="m46.5 26.5 2.5 2.5-2.5 2.5" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="m46.5 32.5 2.5 2.5-2.5 2.5" pathLength="1" />
      </>
    )
  }

  {
    /* 03 — the knowledge graph. A hub, six satellites around it, and nine edges
       — six radial, three between neighbours — so what it draws is a network
       and not a star. The layout is planar on purpose: no edge crosses another,
       every one of them leaves one circumference and stops on the next, and
       nothing runs off into the box. */
    name === "graph" && (
      <>
        <circle cx="32" cy="32" r="6.5" pathLength="1" />
        <circle cx="32" cy="9" r="4" pathLength="1" />
        <circle cx="51.9" cy="20.5" r="4" pathLength="1" />
        <circle cx="51.9" cy="43.5" r="4" pathLength="1" />
        <circle cx="32" cy="55" r="4" pathLength="1" />
        <circle cx="12.1" cy="43.5" r="4" pathLength="1" />
        <circle cx="12.1" cy="20.5" r="4" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="M32 25.5V13" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="m37.6 28.8 10.9-6.3" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="m37.6 35.2 10.9 6.3" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="M32 38.5V51" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="m26.4 35.2-10.9 6.3" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="m26.4 28.8-10.9-6.3" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="m35.5 11 12.9 7.5" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="m48.4 45.5-12.9 7.5" pathLength="1" />
        <path d="M12.1 39.5v-15" pathLength="1" />
      </>
    )
  }
</svg>
